To sync 6 different laptops with a central website, the system uses a "Hub and Spoke" architecture. Here is exactly how they connect and stay in sync:

1. The "Central Hub" (Your Server)
One laptop (or a cloud server) acts as the Main Controller. It runs:

The FastAPI Backend (port 7860): This is the "brain" that holds the database and handles WebSocket connections.
The Dashboard Website (port 3000): This is what the public and admins see.
2. The "Spokes" (6 Station Laptops)
The other 6 laptops do not need to run the full code. They simply open a browser and go to your server's IP (e.g., http://192.168.1.100:3000).

3. How the Sync Works:
Real-Time Data (WebSockets): Every laptop opens a persistent "pipe" (WebSocket) to the central server.
When Laptop #1 saves a node, it sends data to the server.
The server instantly broadcasts that change to the Mosaic Display on the central website.
IP Identification: The Central Hub detects the IP address of each connecting laptop.
If a laptop with IP 192.168.1.101 connects, the server looks at its station registry and says: "Aha! This is STATION_01."
It then shows that station's CPU and temperature on the master dashboard.
Session Persistence:
As you build on a station laptop, the server saves your work into a database.
If you close the laptop and move to another one, you can "Unlock" with your code, and the central server will sync your files to that new station